TRANSLATED=geometry('Figure 8.20','Figure 8.20 — center C(h,0), diameter endpoints h−a,h+a and right angle at P',[path(ellipse(270,180,100,100)),path([(70,180),(420,180)]),path([(80,290),(80,45)]),path([(170,180),(210,100),(370,180)]),path([(205.527864,108.944272),(214.472136,113.416408),(218.944272,104.472136)])],[(74,35,'y'),(424,186,'x'),(58,203,'O'),(147,203,'A'),(377,203,'B'),(257,202,'C(h,0)'),(200,91,'P'),(210,133,'90°')],points=[{'x':270,'y':180}],size=(460,330))
RADIAL=geometry('Figure 8.21','Figure 8.21 — tangent at the outer endpoint P of the radial segment CP',[path(ellipse(235,155,90,90)),path([(75,295),(75,35)]),path([(45,270),(420,270)]),path([(325,45),(325,260)]),path([(235,155),(325,155)])],[(67,25,'y'),(427,277,'x'),(52,297,'O'),(190,147,'C(−g,−f)'),(333,159,'P(x₁,y₁)')],points=[{'x':235,'y':155}],size=(480,330))
PAGES=[page('123_page_1.jpg',237,[heading('Example 18'),paragraph('Show that the angle in the semicircle of the circle (x−h)²+y² = a² is a right-angle.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The circle equation with center C(h,0) is (x−h)²+y² = a². If P(x₁,y₁) is any point on the semicircle and AB is fixed as the diameter of the given circle on the x-axis, then (x₁−h)²+y₁² = a². The coordinates of A and B are respectively:'),eq(r'OA=OC-AC=h-a\ \Rightarrow\ A(h-a,0)'),eq(r'OB=OA+AB=(h-a)+2a=h+a\ \Rightarrow\ B(h+a,0)'),TRANSLATED,paragraph('Join PA and PB that develops a right angle ∠APB. The angle ∠APB is a right angle, if AP and BP are perpendicular. They are perpendicular, if the product of their slopes equals −1:'),eq(r'm_1m_2=\frac{y_1}{x_1-h+a}\cdot\frac{y_1}{x_1-h-a}=\frac{y_1^2}{(x_1-h+a)(x_1-h-a)}'),eq(r'=\frac{y_1^2}{(x_1-h)^2-a^2}=\frac{y_1^2}{-y_1^2}=-1,\quad y_1^2=a^2-(x_1-h)^2'),eq(r'm_1=\frac{y_1-0}{x_1-(h-a)}=\frac{y_1}{x_1-h+a},\quad m_2=\frac{y_1-0}{x_1-(h+a)}=\frac{y_1}{x_1-h-a}'),paragraph('Thus, the angle ∠APB = 90° is right angle.'),note('Require a > 0 and P distinct from the diameter endpoints. The ray dot product (A−P)·(B−P) = (x₁−h)²+y₁²−a² proves perpendicularity without dividing by endpoint coordinate differences. At either endpoint the angle is undefined.'),
 heading('8.4.7 The Perpendicular at the outer end of radial segment is tangent to the circle'),paragraph('The circle equation with center C(−g,−f) is:'),eq(r'x^2+y^2+2gx+2fy+c=0','i'),paragraph('If P(x₁,y₁) is a point on the circle and C(−g,−f) is the center of the circle (i), then CP is the radial segment of the circle. The equation of the tangent line on the circle (i) at point P is:'),RADIAL,eq(r'xx_1+yy_1+g(x+x_1)+f(y+y_1)+c=0'),eq(r'x(x_1+g)+y(y_1+f)+gx_1+fy_1+c=0'),paragraph('whose slope is'),eq(r'm_1=-\frac{x_1+g}{y_1+f},\quad m_2=\frac{y_1+f}{x_1+g},\quad\mathrm{slope\ of}\ CP'),paragraph('The perpendicular at the outer end of the radial segment CP is tangent to the circle (i) if the product of the slopes of the radial segment CP and the line of the outer end of the radial segment CP is −1:'),eq(r'm_1m_2=-\frac{x_1+g}{y_1+f}\cdot\frac{y_1+f}{x_1+g}=-1'),paragraph('Thus, the perpendicular at the outer end P of the radial segment CP is tangent to the circle (i).'),note('For a genuine circle, the radius vector P−C is nonzero. The unique line through P perpendicular to it has distance r from C and hence meets the circle at exactly P, proving tangency. This distance proof includes horizontal and vertical radii, which make one of the printed slope quotients undefined.')]),
 page('123_page_2.jpg',238,[heading('Remember'),paragraph('The tangent line is perpendicular to the radial segment, if the radial segment is the segment through the point of contact of the tangent and the center of the circle.'),paragraph('If a line is perpendicular to the tangent of the circle at the point of contact, then it passes through the center of the circle.'),heading('Example 19'),paragraph('Show that the perpendicular at the outer end point P(1,1) of the radial segment is tangent to the circle x²+y²−13x−5y+16 = 0.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The circle equation with center C(13/2,5/2) is x²+y²−13x−5y+16 = 0. The equation of the tangent line on the given circle at a point P(1,1) is:'),eq(r'xx_1+yy_1+g(x+x_1)+f(y+y_1)+c=0'),eq(r'x(1)+y(1)-\frac{13}2(x+1)+\frac52(y+1)+16=0,\quad P(1,1)'),eq(r'11x+3y-16=0'),paragraph('whose slope is m₁ = −11/3 and the slope of the radial segment CP is'),eq(r'm_2=\frac{1-5/2}{1-13/2}=\frac3{11}'),paragraph('The product of the slopes of the tangent to the circle and the radial segment CP is m₁m₂ = (−11/3)(3/11) = −1. Thus, the perpendicular at the outer end P of the radial segment is tangent to the given circle at point P.'),note('The displayed substitution prints +5/2 for f although the circle coefficient is −5y, so f = −5/2. The next equation 11x+3y−16 = 0 has a further constant error and misses P(1,1). Correct substitution gives 11x+3y−14 = 0. The learning solution checks contact, perpendicularity and center-to-line distance against the original circle.'),
 heading('Exercise 8.3'),paragraph('1. If A(2,2) and B(3,1) are the end points of the chord AB of x²+y²−4x−2y+4 = 0, show that (a) the line from the center is perpendicular to AB and also bisects AB; (b) the line from the center to the midpoint of AB is perpendicular to AB.'),paragraph('2. If A(0,0) and B(0,3) are the end points of the chord AB of x²+y²+4x−5y = 0, show that (a) the line from the center is perpendicular to AB and also bisects AB; (b) the line from the center to the midpoint of AB is perpendicular to AB.'),paragraph('3. Show that chords AB and DE are equidistant from C(0,0): (a) x²+y² = 4, endpoints A(−2,0), B(0,2), D(0,2), E(2,0); (b) x²+y² = 16, endpoints A(−4,0), B(0,4), D(0,4), E(4,0).'),paragraph('4. Show that the angle subtended by the minor arc AB is two times the angle subtended in the major arc: (a) x²+y² = 9, A(2,√5), B(2,−√5).'),note('Exercise 8.3 continues on printed 239. In Question 2, B(0,3) is not on the stated circle: its polynomial value is −6. Its line segment with A therefore is not the claimed circle chord; the learning solution explains the failed midpoint-perpendicular claim explicitly.')])]
for p in PAGES:
 p.update(title='CONICS-I',visualStatus='Complete translated-semicircle and radial-tangent proofs, Examples 18–19 and source Exercise 8.3 opening',alternateVerification={'pdf':'Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf','pdfPage':123,'side':'left' if p['page']==237 else 'right','reason':'All translated coordinates, incorrect f sign and off-circle Exercise 8.3 point visually checked'})
EXAMPLES=[]
for n,q,steps,ans,p in [
 ('18','Show the angle in a semicircle of (x−h)²+y² = a² is a right angle.',['Take a > 0. The center is C(h,0) and diameter endpoints are A(h−a,0), B(h+a,0).','Let P(x,y) lie on the circle, distinct from A and B, so (x−h)²+y² = a².','The two angle rays are A−P = (h−a−x,−y) and B−P = (h+a−x,−y).','Their dot product is (h−x−a)(h−x+a)+y² = (x−h)²−a²+y².','The circle equation makes that dot product zero. Both rays are nonzero, so they are perpendicular.','Therefore ∠APB = 90° on either open semicircle. The endpoint exclusions are essential because the angle is undefined there.'],'∠APB = 90° for every non-endpoint P on either semicircle.',237),
 ('19','Show the perpendicular to the radius at P(1,1) is tangent to x²+y²−13x−5y+16 = 0.',['Check the circumference condition: 1+1−13−5+16 = 0.','The center is C(13/2,5/2), with squared radius 169/4+25/4−16 = 65/2.','The nonzero radius vector P−C is (−11/2,−3/2). A perpendicular line through P satisfies 11(x−1)+3(y−1) = 0.','Thus the true tangent is 11x+3y−14 = 0. It contains P because 11+3−14 = 0.','A tangent direction is (3,−11), whose dot product with the radius is (−33+33)/2 = 0.','The line value at C is 11·13/2+3·5/2−14 = 79−14 = 65. The center-to-line distance is 65/√130, whose square is 65/2, exactly the radius squared.','The printed line 11x+3y−16 = 0 has value −2 at P and squared center-distance 3969/130, different from 65/2. It is not the requested tangent. This constant error is separate from the incorrect positive f sign in the preceding source substitution.'],'Correct tangent 11x+3y−14 = 0; the printed 11x+3y−16 = 0 misses P and is not tangent.',238)]:
 EXAMPLES.append({'num':n,'title':'Radial and Semicircle Properties','question':q,'steps':steps,'answer':ans,'progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':p,'jpg':'123_page_1.jpg' if p==237 else '123_page_2.jpg','verification':'Complete source coefficients and independent contact/distance checks reviewed'}],**({'sourceQuestionBlocks':[TRANSLATED]} if n=='18' else {})})
